The `DependencyInjector` class should have the following methods:
1. `bind(Class<?> clazz)`: This method should bind the target class to the `DependencyInjector` so that it can be instantiated and dependencies can be injected into it.
2. `getInstance(Class<?> clazz)`: This method should return an instance of the target class with its dependencies injected.

The target class will have methods annotated with `@Provides` to indicate the types of dependencies it can provide. For example:
```java
public class MyModule {
    @Provides
    public boolean provideBoolean() {
        return true;
    }

    @Provides
    public float provideFloat() {
        return 1.0f;
    }
}
```

Your task is to implement the `DependencyInjector` class to support the functionality described above.

code:
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class DependencyInjector {
    private Map<Class<?>, Object> bindings = new HashMap<>();

    public void bind(Class<?> clazz) {
        try {
            Object instance = clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
            bindings.put(clazz, instance);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public <T> T getInstance(Class<T> clazz) {
        Object instance = bindings.get(clazz);
        if (instance != null) {
            injectDependencies(instance);
            return clazz.cast(instance);
        }
        return null;
    }

    private void injectDependencies(Object instance) {
        Class<?> clazz = instance.getClass();
        for (Method method : clazz.getDeclaredMethods()) {
            if (method.isAnnotationPresent(Provides.class)) {
                Class<?> returnType = method.getReturnType();
                Object dependency = getInstance(returnType);
                try {
                    method.invoke(instance, dependency);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

